# Checker profiles and config used by `haskoki-demo check` / `compare`

Files here ship inside the demo image
(`/opt/haskoki/examples/release/pkcs11-check/`) and are load-bearing:
the entrypoint sources `profiles.conf` and copies `backend-memory.toml`
into each run dir. Profiles are FROZEN (R4); only the compare subset
stays provisional (R5).

## Profiles (`profiles.conf`)

- `smoke`: offline marker + `-k 'interface or slot or digest or
  profiles'` (743 tests; zero findings direct and proxied as measured
  for R3).
- `full`: offline marker over the whole suite, no `-k` selection
  (11003 tests; 27 findings direct, proxy 40 stable findings
  (39 failed + 1 crash) + 0–2 timing-flaky failures, as measured
  for R4 on checker 0.2.3 — asserted by
  `scripts/test-demo-image.sh`).
- The offline marker is the CI fast-lane marker verbatim: vector-data
  lanes (`wycheproof`/`acvp`/`cctv`) need fetched data that the offline
  demo image does not carry; `stress`/`fuzz`/`slow` stay out of the
  interactive lanes.

## Backend (`backend-memory.toml`)

Memory store, real-crypto OpenSSL engine, no synthetic fallback, trace
off. The checker manages its own keys per test; direct lanes export it
as `HASKOKI_CONFIG`, proxy lanes hand it to the daemon. PINs come from
`P11TEST_PIN`/`P11TEST_SO_PIN` (PUBLIC fixtures 1234/5678, never logged).

## What R4/R5 freeze (do not treat R3 values as final)

- R4: profile selections (smoke/full scope), the disposition of every
  R3-measured finding (module gap vs oracle drift vs accepted), the
  frozen checker reports and release-results docs.
- R5: the compare parity-subset definition (R3 compares all), proxy
  version qualification (R3 builds the regression pin by default),
  honest-comparison docs.

## Reproducing the measurements

```
docker run --rm -v "$PWD/out:/out" haskoki-demo:0.3.0.0 \
  check --mode direct --profile smoke   # exit 0
docker run --rm -v "$PWD/out:/out" haskoki-demo:0.3.0.0 \
  check --mode direct --profile full    # exit 1, 27 findings listed
```

Each run keeps `results.json` (checker-native), `report.jsonl`
(per-test records), `report.json` (entrypoint verdict) and
`findings.txt` (stable `outcome test-id` rows) under its run dir.
